From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: explorer: the desktop's Copy, Cut and Paste reach the clipboard

David: copy and paste did not work. A file copied on the desktop (right-click
> Copy) could not be pasted anywhere. The desktop's icon menu ran under
CoInitialize alone, and shell32's Copy uses OleSetClipboard, which needs OLE;
an OleUninitialize afterwards would take the clipboard's formats with it.
The desktop's thread now keeps OLE for its life, as Windows' desktop does.
The desktop also gets Paste in its menu and Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V and
Delete on its icons, as on Windows.

Gate: make test-desktop (Ctrl+C on an icon, read from another program;
Ctrl+V on the desktop).
--- a/programs/explorer/desktop.c
+++ b/programs/explorer/desktop.c
@@ -1072,6 +1072,19 @@
     return TRUE;
 }
 
+/* Stained Glass OS: OLE for the desktop's thread, for good. An icon's Copy
+ * and Cut put it on the clipboard with OleSetClipboard, which failed under
+ * CoInitialize alone; and OleUninitialize afterwards took the clipboard's
+ * formats with it: a file copied on the desktop could not be pasted anywhere.
+ * Windows' desktop keeps OLE for its thread's life. */
+static HRESULT sg_ole_init(void)
+{
+    static HRESULT hr = E_FAIL;
+
+    if (FAILED( hr )) hr = OleInitialize( NULL );
+    return hr;
+}
